  "estimatedMinutes": null,
  "statement": "Let \\(P(x)=x^{3}-\\frac{3}{2} x^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{4}\\). Let \\(P^{[1]}(x)=P(x)\\), and for \\(n \\geq 1\\), let \\(P^{[n+1]}(x)=\\) \\(P^{[n]}(P(x))\\). Evaluate \\(\\int_{0}^{1} P^{[2004]}(x) d x\\).",
  "answerChoices": [],
  "answer": {
    "letter": null,
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.hmmt-feb-calculus-2004-p10.hmmt",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "\\(1 / 2\\)\nBy Note that \\(P(1-x)=1-P(x)\\). It follows easily by induction that \\(P^{[k]}(1-x)=\\) \\(1-P^{[k]}(x)\\) for all positive integers \\(k\\). Hence\n\n\\[\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{1} P^{[2004]}(x) d x & =\\int_{0}^{1} 1-P^{[2004]}(1-x) d x \\\\\n& =1-\\int_{0}^{1} P^{[2004]}(1-x) d x \\\\\n& =1-\\int_{0}^{1} P^{[2004]}(u) d u \\quad(u=1-x)\n\\end{aligned}\\]\n\nHence \\(\\int_{0}^{1} P^{[2004]}(x) d x=1 / 2\\).",
